    "language": "en",
    "md5": "742154da9ae4926933a27428cdaedf00",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1609,
    "chunk_text": "One solace for the government in this case is that the Opposition, which usually makes a huge fuss at the drop of a hat, has been notably silent on these recent incidents. No party in Opposition has been expressing their views on these incidents or alleged incidents. Nor have they shown any interest in discussing the allegations against the National Child Protection Authority (NCPA) and the police over their lethargic attitude towards these incidents,\u00a0 which are a main issue debated in the social media. This indifference or\u00a0 fear at the political level indicates the level of sensitivity the incidents in question carry.\n\nThe relationship between the state and\u00a0 religion has been contentious for centuries in many countries, despite the notion of \u201cSeparation of religion from the state\u201d, realisation of it on the ground has always been a question. In some cases,\u00a0 states have been built with religion as their foundation in countries such as Afghanistan, Iran, Vatican and Israel.\n\nIn fact, religion maintains a profound influence on state affairs globally. Countries like United Kingdom, Denmark, Saudi Arabia and Sri Lanka officially endorse a specific religion, granting it privileges, funding, or a primary role in constitutional matters. Although nations such as United States, Turkey and India maintain a formal separation between religion and state, culture and politics still heavily intersect in those countries. In India, Hindutva has almost taken over the government. The 2019 Supreme Court judgment on the Babri Masjid case is a perfectexample of Hindutva influence on the judiciary."
  },
