    "md5": "123cc0276c261e89df22e61439dbc7aa",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2014,
    "chunk_text": "In the last count, there are at least 930 suspected cases and 349 deaths in the DRC, and seven cases with one death in Uganda. This region is a major commercial hub and mining centre and is densely populated. Two million people had been displaced and 10 million face acute hunger here. Armed conflict has intensified over the past two months, making it difficult to get health assistance to the area.\n\nOn May 17, the WHO declared the current Ebola epidemic a \u201cPublic Health Emergency of International Concern\u201d, and days later, warned that it could be months before the outbreak was contained.\n\nThe Sudan virus has caused several outbreaks around South Sudan, Uganda, and the DRC. The Tai Forest virus is very rare \u2014 there\u2019s only one report of a single infection in Cote d\u2019Ivoire in 1994.\n\nThe Bundibugyo virus\u2014the cause of the ongoing outbreak\u2014has been reported as the cause of two previous outbreaks, with the current outbreak being the third. All have been around the DRC and Uganda border.\n\nAccording to Maya Brownstein of the Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health, Ebola is a \u201czoonotic disease\u201d - that originates in animals and can then, in what\u2019s called a \u201cspillover\u201d, be transmitted to humans.\n\nEbola is carried by fruit bats. People get infected either through contact with fruit bats or through contact with nonhuman primates that were themselves infected through contact with fruit bats. Once spillover occurs, human-to-human transmission begins.\n\nThe virus can be transmitted from an infected person\u2014or someone who recently died from the virus\u2014when their bodily fluids, such as urine, saliva, faeces, vomit, breast milk, and sweat, come into direct contact with another person\u2019s mucus membranes or a break in their skin.\n\nPeople caring for those sick with Ebola, whether clinicians or family members, are at serious risk of infection. Transmission is also associated with funeral rites that involve washing and preparing for burial the bodies of those who died of Ebola."
