    "md5": "667847a6bb272e15c6c755129664ae88",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1922,
    "chunk_text": "Meridian Talent counters AI-led hiring with human-first recruitment model\n\nFrom left: Meridian Creative Solutions funder and Director Viraj Adhihetty and Meridian Talent co-founder and CEO Az Da Rosa\n\nMeridian Talent, the specialist recruitment division of Meridian Creative Solutions, led by Az Da Rosa, argues that hiring accuracy should be treated as a commercial priority, not just a recruitment target.\n\nAs hiring becomes increasingly speed-driven and automated, businesses are being pushed to treat hiring as a race to fill roles rather than a decision about long-term fit and performance.\n\nTechnology leaders are under mounting pressure to hire quickly but the commercial risk of getting a hire wrong is substantial.\n\nGlobal research frequently places the cost of a bad hire at 30 percent of first-year salary at minimum, rising materially for senior roles. At the same time, specialised technology roles commonly take weeks to fill, increasing delivery risk, team strain and opportunity cost. Meridian Talent\u2019s model is built to reduce those risks through alignment-first, human-led assessment, where technology supports judgment rather than replacing it.\n\n\u201cAI tools can be useful at handling volume but they cannot reliably measure the things that determine whether someone will thrive in a technical team,\u201d said Meridian Talent co-founder and CEO Az Da Rosa.\n\n\u201cIn my experience, the hires that fail are rarely just a skills problem. They\u2019re an alignment problem: working style, communication under pressure, delivery mindset and cultural fit. Our job is to surface that early, so hiring feels less like a gamble and more like a partnership.\u201d\n\nMeridian Creative Solutions founder and Director Viraj Adhihetty said the goal is not to reject automation but to restore context to a process that has become overly keyword-driven.\n\n\u201cAutomation and AI absolutely have a place in modern hiring,\u201d he said."
  },
  {
